WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:05.292
Alex declines a crowded birthday party, describing themself as an introvert.

00:05.701 --> 00:09.795
The next afternoon, Alex talks for hours at a small repair club.

00:10.203 --> 00:12.219
A friend sees a contradiction.

00:12.588 --> 00:17.677
But size, noise, familiarity and purpose differ between those settings.

00:18.086 --> 00:22.117
A preference for quieter gatherings does not require Alex to dislike

00:22.129 --> 00:23.354
every kind of company.

00:24.372 --> 00:28.823
A personality trait is a tendency in how someone thinks, feels or

00:28.836 --> 00:30.418
behaves across occasions.

00:30.801 --> 00:33.161
It does not describe everything they do.

00:33.569 --> 00:38.059
The Big Five describes five broad scales, not five types of person.

00:38.442 --> 00:43.008
Being less outgoing than a friend does not decide how organised you are, whether you

00:43.021 --> 00:45.789
are honest, or which opportunities you should receive.

00:46.830 --> 00:51.090
William Fleeson asked people to report their current behaviour several times

00:51.103 --> 00:52.544
a day in three studies.

00:52.914 --> 00:56.537
People varied considerably between occasions while their average

00:56.550 --> 00:58.029
patterns were more stable.

00:58.438 --> 01:02.328
That helps explain how someone can be less outgoing overall and still

01:02.341 --> 01:03.706
have talkative moments.

01:04.114 --> 01:08.068
It does not establish Alex’s lifetime pattern from one example.

01:09.080 --> 01:13.621
Alex prefers small groups with a shared activity gives you something to use when

01:13.634 --> 01:14.909
planning an invitation.

01:15.318 --> 01:17.908
Alex is an introvert gives less detail.

01:18.277 --> 01:21.287
Ask when the behaviour happens and when it does not.

01:21.696 --> 01:25.624
If you remember only declined parties, you miss the repair club.

01:26.033 --> 01:30.892
A few examples can improve a conversation without becoming a personality test.

01:31.913 --> 01:36.365
In a different part of Alex’s life, reading is often displaced by picking up

01:36.378 --> 01:37.538
the phone on the sofa.

01:37.922 --> 01:41.991
A habit is a learned tendency to respond fairly automatically when a

01:42.004 --> 01:43.777
familiar situation occurs.

01:44.146 --> 01:48.470
Repetition in a familiar setting can make an action more automatic.

01:48.879 --> 01:52.743
It does not necessarily mean Alex newly decides each evening that

01:52.756 --> 01:54.249
reading is unimportant.

01:55.288 --> 01:58.171
Watch what happens on evenings when Alex reads.

01:58.541 --> 02:02.317
Perhaps the book is already open or the phone is charging elsewhere.

02:02.687 --> 02:06.207
But Alex might also be waiting for an important message, or

02:06.220 --> 02:07.763
struggling with poor lighting.

02:08.159 --> 02:11.348
Moving the phone would not solve every possible problem.

02:11.731 --> 02:16.106
Ask what is happening and what Alex actually wants before choosing a change.

02:17.122 --> 02:21.713
Alex chooses to put the book beside the sofa and leave the phone on a nearby shelf for

02:21.726 --> 02:24.826
three evenings, with important calls still audible.

02:25.236 --> 02:30.236
This is a modest change to Alex’s own routine, not a test imposed by a friend.

02:30.618 --> 02:33.080
More reading would be useful information.

02:33.449 --> 02:37.187
Discovering that this is the wrong time to read could be useful too.

02:38.205 --> 02:39.991
Here is a separate example.

02:40.374 --> 02:43.295
A housemate repeatedly leaves a door unlocked.

02:43.664 --> 02:47.504
Calling it forgetfulness or a habit does not make the door secure.

02:47.913 --> 02:52.772
Agree a practical way to keep it locked, considering everyone’s access and safety,

02:53.078 --> 02:54.520
then check whether it works.

02:54.928 --> 02:58.869
You can need a reliable result without proving why someone forgot.

02:59.913 --> 03:04.531
When you use a fixed label about yourself or someone else, ask: When does this

03:04.543 --> 03:06.278
happen, and when does it not?

03:06.675 --> 03:09.111
Write one example and one exception.

03:09.480 --> 03:13.498
Choose a small change that fits the person’s needs, and review it.

03:13.907 --> 03:18.601
One good day is not a personality transformation; one missed evening is not

03:18.613 --> 03:19.914
a verdict on character.
